sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: Pyramids of the same height with triangular bases are to one another as their bases.
----
proof:

Let there be pyramids of the same height with triangular bases {ABC} and {DEF} and vertices {G} and {H}.

I say that the base {ABC} is to the base {DEF} as the pyramid {ABCG} is to the pyramid {DEFH}.

For, if the pyramid {ABCG} is not to the pyramid {DEFH} as the base {ABC} is to the base {DEF}, then the base {ABC} is to the base {DEF} as the pyramid {ABCG} is either to some solid less than the pyramid {DEFH} or to a greater solid.

<center>
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="550" height="280" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="XII.5 diagram" src="propXII5.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // A double reductio on the XII.3/XII.4 figure. Case 1: W less than DEFH.
    // DEFH is divided (XII.3) until its leftover pyramids are less than the
    // excess of DEFH over W (X.1), so its prisms exceed W; ABCG divided alike
    // gives, through XII.4, V.11 and V.16, W greater than those prisms too —
    // impossible. Case 2, W greater, inverts to case 1 (XII.2's lemma).
    //
    // Joyce's figure is kept whole: the division is XII.3's work and is
    // assumed here, so the walk lights it rather than redraws it. Only the
    // solid W is held back until the proof posits it, and withdrawn when
    // each case is refuted. A polyhedron draws no highlight, so everything
    // the prose names gets a transparent twin: the bases ABC, DEF; faceless
